    "content": "{\"files\":[{\"content\":\"#include <cstdio>\\n#include <vector>\\n\\nstatic inline int readInt() {\\n    int x = 0;\\n    char c = getchar_unlocked();\\n    bool neg = false;\\n    while (c < '0' || c > '9') {\\n        if (c == '-') neg = true;\\n        c = getchar_unlocked();\\n    }\\n    while (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {\\n        x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\\n        c = getchar_unlocked();\\n    }\\n    return neg ? -x : x;\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int n = readInt();\\n    int s = readInt();\\n\\n    const size_t rowLen = (size_t)n;\\n    std::vector<int> buf((size_t)(s + 1) * n);\\n    std::vector<long long> col(n, 0);\\n\\n    // Read first S rows and accumulate column sums.\\n    for (int r = 0; r < s; ++r) {\\n        int* row = &buf[(size_t)r * n];\\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; ++c) {\\n            int v = readInt();\\n            row[c] = v;\\n            col[c] += v;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    long long cur = 0;\\n    for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += col[j];\\n    long long mx = cur;\\n\\n    const int nrows = n - s + 1;\\n    const int ncols = n - s + 1;\\n    const int ringSize = s + 1;\\n\\n    int oldSlot = 0;\\n    int newSlot = s % ringSize; // slot for row index s when i=0\\n    const int* pold = buf.data() + (size_t)oldSlot * n;\\n    int* pnew = buf.data() + (size_t)newSlot * n;\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < nrows; ++i) {\\n        // Horizontal sliding window over columns.\\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\\n        for (int j = 1; j < ncols; ++j) {\\n            cur += col[j + s - 1] - col[j - 1];\\n            if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (i + 1 < nrows) {\\n            long long deltaFirstS = 0;\\n\\n            // Read the entering row and update column sums for first S columns.\\n            int c = 0;\\n            for (; c + 3 < s; c += 4) {\\n                int v0 = readInt();\\n                int v1 = readInt();\\n                int v2 = readInt();\\n                int v3 = readInt();\\n                pnew[c]     = v0;\\n                pnew[c + 1] = v1;\\n                pnew[c + 2] = v2;\\n                pnew[c + 3] = v3;\\n                long long d0 = (long long)v0 - pold[c];\\n                long long d1 = (long long)v1 - pold[c + 1];\\n                long long d2 = (long long)v2 - pold[c + 2];\\n                long long d3 = (long long)v3 - pold[c + 3];\\n                col[c]     += d0;\\n                col[c + 1] += d1;\\n                col[c + 2] += d2;\\n                col[c + 3] += d3;\\n                deltaFirstS += d0 + d1 + d2 + d3;\\n            }\\n            for (; c < s; ++c) {\\n                int v = readInt();\\n                pnew[c] = v;\\n                long long d = (long long)v - pold[c];\\n                col[c] += d;\\n                deltaFirstS += d;\\n            }\\n\\n            // Remaining columns.\\n            for (; c + 3 < n; c += 4) {\\n                int v0 = readInt();\\n                int v1 = readInt();\\n                int v2 = readInt();\\n                int v3 = readInt();\\n                pnew[c]     = v0;\\n                pnew[c + 1] = v1;\\n                pnew[c + 2] = v2;\\n                pnew[c + 3] = v3;\\n                col[c]     += (long long)v0 - pold[c];\\n                col[c + 1] += (long long)v1 - pold[c + 1];\\n                col[c + 2] += (long long)v2 - pold[c + 2];\\n                col[c + 3] += (long long)v3 - pold[c + 3];\\n            }\\n            for (; c < n; ++c) {\\n                int v = readInt();\\n                pnew[c] = v;\\n                col[c] += (long long)v - pold[c];\\n            }\\n\\n            cur += deltaFirstS;\\n\\n            // Advance ring slots for the next row transition.\\n            if (++oldSlot == ringSize) oldSlot = 0;\\n            if (++newSlot == ringSize) newSlot = 0;\\n            pold = buf.data() + (size_t)oldSlot * n;\\n            pnew = buf.data() + (size_t)newSlot * n;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    printf(\\\"%lld\\\\n\\\", mx);\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n\",\"path\":\"src/solution.cpp\"}],\"hypothesis\":\"Streaming the matrix through a small (S+1)-row circular buffer while fusing input parsing with column-sum updates and unrolling the update loop reduces memory traffic and improves cache locality, beating previous full-matrix or larger-ring implementations.\",\"notes\":\"Replaced the NxN storage with an (S+1)-row ring buffer, so only rows in the current vertical window plus one incoming row are resident. Input reading for each new row is fused directly into the column-sum update, eliminating separate passes over matrix data. Manual slot-pointer wrapping avoids modulo arithmetic per transition, and 4-way unrolling of the read/update loops improves instruction-level parallelism on the ARM64 target. The horizontal sliding-window scan remains unchanged and sequential; no OpenMP overhead is introduced at this problem size.\",\"sources_kept\":[],\"transformations\":[\"Replace full NxN matrix storage with an (S+1)-row circular buffer to shrink working set and improve cache locality\",\"Fuse input parsing of each entering row with column-sum updates, removing extra passes over stored data\",\"Use increment-and-wrap slot pointers instead of modulo arithmetic for ring indexing\",\"Unroll the read/update loops by four to increase memory-level parallelism\"]}",
    "thinking": "We need to propose one bounded HPC source-code experiment. Role A: memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking. We have current source at src/solution.cpp. Need to edit only that file. Must preserve correctness for all sizes/seeds/repeated calls. Target ARM64 4 cores, OMP_NUM_THREADS=4.\n\nWe need propose one candidate transformation relevant to role A. Current implementation: reads full NxN matrix into vector<int> a (row-major). Computes column sums of first S rows. Then sliding window over columns using col array and row updates by adding entering row minus exiting row. In non-OpenMP path, it does horizontal sliding with cur update per j, then vertical update for next i. This is O(N^2) after reading.\n\nWe can improve memory locality/data layout/blocking. Observations: current best around 0.0113s (aec6b1...). Baseline 0.1337s. We need beat previous results maybe. Role A specifically memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking.\n\nPotential transformations:\n- Avoid storing full matrix? But we already read all input; could process streaming with ring buffer of S rows to reduce memory footprint and improve cache. However current code stores NxN ints (up to 8000^2 = 64M ints ~256MB) which may exceed L3 but okay. Streaming would avoid huge allocation, better locality.\n- Use circular buffer of only S+1? Actually need row i+s entering and row i exiting; can keep ring of S rows plus current? We process sequentially: for each new row after first S, update col sums by adding new row minus old row. Need access to old row (i) and new row (i+S). If we store only last S+1 rows in a circular buffer, memory footprint O(S*N), much smaller. This improves cache locality.\n- Also fuse input reading with computation? We can read first S rows into ring, compute initial col sums; then for each subsequent row, update and slide horizontally. But horizontal sliding needs all columns of current window; we can do after updating col array. That's fine.\n\nCurrent code reads entire matrix before computing. Streaming would reduce memory traffic and improve locality. This is role A relevant (data layout/blocking). Let's design carefully.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Read n, s.\n- Allocate ring buffer rows: vector<int> buf(S * N) maybe S*N ints; if S can be up to N-1 < 8000, max ~64M ints same as full but average smaller. Actually worst-case S=N-1 => almost full matrix anyway. But typical? We don't know benchmark inputs. Could still help.\n- Use modulo indexing or pointer wrap. For performance, use ring of size s+1 maybe to handle entering row and exiting row simultaneously. Let's think.\n\nWe need maintain col[j] = sum over rows i..i+s-1 for current top-left row i. Initially i=0: read first S rows into buffer positions 0..S-1 (row-major within each row). Compute col sums.\nThen horizontal sliding across columns for this i: compute max submatrix sum among all column windows at fixed top row i. We can use prefix or incremental cur as current code does in non-OpenMP path. The existing non-OpenMP path is efficient and correct; we'll keep that logic but adapt to ring buffer.\n\nAfter finishing row i, if i+1 < nrows, need update col for next i: add row i+s (entering) minus row i (exiting). With ring buffer of size s+1? We can store rows in a circular array of capacity maybe 2*s? Simpler: allocate vector<int> buf((s+1)*n) and use index modulo (s+1)? But when we need exiting row i, it's at position i % (s+1); entering row i+s is also at same position after wrap if ring size s+1 because (i+s) mod (s+1) = (i-1) mod (s+1), not same. Actually with capacity s+1, positions cycle every s+1 rows; when we read new row r, store at r % (s+1). At step i, exiting row index i is stored at i%(s+1); entering row index i+s is stored at (i+s)%(s+1) = (i-1)%(s+1), which was previously occupied by row i-1. But we need both simultaneously; if we overwrite before reading old, problem. We can read new row into position after using exiting? Better use capacity 2*s or s*2 to avoid overlap. Or process differently: maintain buffer of last S rows only and when moving down, replace oldest with newest. Need access to entering row (new) and exiting row (old). If we store ring size S, at step i the exiting row is at position i%S; entering row will be stored at same position after reading? Actually if we read new row r = i+S, it should overwrite position r%S = (i+S)%S = i%S, which currently holds exiting row. So we need to compute delta using old values before overwriting. We can do: for each column c, col[c] += newrow[c] - oldrow[c]; then store newrow into that slot. That works with ring size S (or maybe S+1 not needed). But careful when i=0 initial rows 0..S-1 stored at positions 0..S-1. At step i=0, exiting row is row 0 at pos 0; entering row is row S, which should overwrite pos 0 after we read it and compute delta. Good.\n\nSo allocate vector<int> buf(S * n) (size_t). Use pointer arithmetic with modulo? Modulo per element expensive. We can use two pointers: for each new row, base = &buf[(r % S)*n]. But r%S computed once per row; then loop over columns using pnew[c] and pexit[c]. That's fine.\n\nHowever reading input streaming: we need read first S rows into buf positions 0..S-1. Then compute initial col sums by summing each column across those rows. Could do while reading? Simpler after reading all S rows, loop j over n and r over s to sum; that's O(S*N) extra but okay (same as before). Or accumulate during read: for each row r, for each c, val=readInt(); buf[r*n+c]=val; col[c]+=val. That avoids second pass. Good.\n\nThen horizontal sliding:\n- Compute cur = sum_{j=0..s-1} col[j]; mx=cur.\n- For i from 0 to nrows-1:\n   - Slide horizontally: for j=1..ncols-1, cur += col[j+s-1] - col[j-1], update mx. This is O(n) per row.\n   - If not last row, read next row r=i+S into buffer at slot (r % S)*n; compute delta = newrow[c]-oldrow[c] for all c, where oldrow is &buf[(i%S)*n]; col[c]+=delta. Also need update cur? In current non-OpenM