1 at slot 1. So old row 0 was already used and can be overwritten safely because we no longer need it (we only need current window rows i..i+S-1). With capacity S+1, the set of slots for current window rows i..i+S-1 are distinct? Let's check: indices r from i to i+S-1 modulo S+1. Since there are S consecutive integers mod S+1, they occupy all except one slot (the missing residue). The entering row i+S has same residue as exiting row i-1? Actually (i+S) mod (S+1) = (i-1) mod (S+1), which is not among current window residues because current window residues are i..i+S-1 mod S+1, which excludes i-1. So entering slot is free/old unused row. Good. Thus capacity S+1 works without temp.\n\nSo allocate buf((S+1)*n). Initial fill rows 0..S-1 at slots r (since r < S+1). Compute col sums.\nLoop:\nfor i=0..nrows-1:\n   horizontal scan using current col and cur.\n   if not last:\n      int slot_new = (i + s) % (s + 1);\n      const int* pold = &buf[(size_t)(i % (s+1)) * n];\n      const int* pnew = &buf[(size_t)slot_new * n]; // this slot currently holds row i-1? Actually at step i, before reading new row, slot_new may contain old data from previous cycle; we will overwrite it with read values. But we need compute delta using pold and newly read values. We can read directly into buf[slot_new] then compute col[c] += buf[slot_new][c] - pold[c]. That's fine because pnew is separate from pold (since slot_new != i%(s+1) as argued). So no temp needed.\n      for c=0..n-1: int v = readInt(); buf[slot_new*n+c]=v; col[c]+=v - pold[c];\n      Also compute delta_first_s sum of first s deltas to update cur. We can accumulate during loop or after. In current code they do separate loops for first s and rest, but we can combine: while reading each c, if c<s add v-pold[c] to delta_first; col[c]+=... This is fine.\n      Then cur += delta_first_s.\n\nBut careful: when i+1 < nrows, the next iteration's horizontal scan starts with updated col and cur. Good.\n\nEdge cases: S could be 0? Problem says