use it.\n\nLet's inspect previous best experiment aec6b1a40d3046d2: hypothesis \"streaming ring-buffer approach already avoids storing full matrix; replacing modulo-based slot arithmetic with increment-and-wrap row pointers, unrolling the read/update loop by four, and using a direct buffer-cursor scanner reduce per-row overhead...\" That likely used ring buffer of S rows? It achieved 0.011319s. Our new idea capacity S+1 avoids temp and uses fused reading; may be similar but maybe slightly better due to fewer passes.\n\nWe need propose one candidate only. Let's implement streaming with ring size S+1, fused read/update, horizontal sliding as current non-OpenMP path (which is already efficient). Also consider loop unrolling for column update? Could add #pragma GCC unroll or manual 4x unroll to improve memory-level parallelism. But role A includes loop order; we can reorder loops: currently vertical update loops over all columns then horizontal scan loops over ncols; that's fine. Maybe process in blocks of columns to improve cache locality for both col and buf? Since col array is size N (up to 8000) fits L1/L2 easily, so no need.\n\nLet's write code carefully.\n\nPseudo:\n\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n\nstatic inline int readInt() { ... same }\n\nint main() {\n    int n = readInt();\n    int s = readInt();\n\n    const size_t rowBytes = (size_t)n;\n    std::vector<int> buf((size_t)(s + 1) * n);\n    std::vector<long long> col(n, 0);\n\n    // Read first S rows and accumulate column sums\n    for (int r = 0; r < s; ++r) {\n        int* row = &buf[(size_t)r * n];\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; ++c) {\n            int v = readInt();\n            row[c] = v;\n            col[c] += v;\n        }\n    }\n\n    long long cur = 0;\n    for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += col[j];\n    long long mx = cur;\n\n    const int nrows = n - s + 1;\n    const int ncols = n - s + 1;\n    const int ringMask? no, use modulo.\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < nrows; ++i) {\n        // horizontal scan\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n        for (int j = 1; j < ncols; ++j) {\n            cur += col[j + s - 1] - col[j - 1];\n            if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n        }\n        if (i + 1 < nrows) {\n            int slotNew = (i + s) % (s + 1);\n            const int* pold = &buf[(size_t)(i % (s + 1)) * n];\n            int* pnew = &buf[(size_t)slotNew * n];\n            long long deltaFirstS = 0;\n            // read new row and update col\n            for (int c = 0; c < s; ++c) {\n                int v = readInt();\n                pnew[c] = v;\n                long long d = (long long)v - pold[c];\n                col[c] += d;\n                deltaFirstS += d;\n            }\n            for (int c = s; c < n; ++c) {\n                int v = readInt();\n                pnew[c] = v;\n                col[c] += (long long)v - pold[c];\n            }\n            cur += deltaFirstS;\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%lld\\n\", mx);\n}\n\nCheck correctness:\n- Initial rows 0..s-1 stored at slots 0..s-1. col sums correct.\n- For i=0, horizontal scan computes max over all column windows for top row 0 using initial col (sum of rows 0..s-1). Good.\n- Then read row s into slotNew = s % (s+1) = s (since s < s+1), which is empty. pold points to row 0 at slot 0. Update col to sum rows 1..s. deltaFirstS updates cur for column window starting at 0: cur was sum col[0..s-1] before; after update, new cur should be sum of updated col[0..s-1]. We add deltaFirstS = sum_{c=0}^{s-1} (new-old). Correct.\n- Next i=1 horizontal scan starts with cur representing window at column 0 for rows 1..s. Then slides horizontally. Good.\n- At end, mx correct.\n\nPotential issue: when s+1 ring size and nrows maybe large; modulo per row is fine but could be optimized by incrementing pointers/wrapping manually. Previous best used increment-and-wrap row pointers to avoid modulo. We can do similar:\n   int slotOld = 0;\n   int slotNew = s % (s+1); // initially for i=0\nBut after each iteration, old slot increments? For next i, exiting row index becomes i+1; its slot is (i+1)%(s+1). Entering row index i+s+1 has slot ((i+1)+s)%(s+1) = (slotNew + 1) % (s+1)? Let's derive:\nAt iteration i, oldSlot = i%(s+1), newSlot=(i+s)%(s+1).\nNext i'=i+1: oldSlot'=(i+1)%(s+1)=oldSlot+1 mod; newSlot'=(i+1+s)%(s+1)=(newSlot+1) mod. So both increment by 1 modulo s+1. We can maintain two ints and wrap with if (++slot == s+1) slot=0. This avoids division/modulo per row.\n\nAlso pold/pnew pointers can be updated by adding n each time, wrapping to base when reaching end of buffer? Since slots are contiguous rows in buf; incrementing pointer by n moves to next row; after last slot (s), wrap to 0. We can maintain int* oldPtr and newPtr similarly.\n\nLet's implement with manual wrap for performance:\n   const size_t rowSize = (size_t)n;\n   std::vector<int> buf((size_t)(s+1)*n);\n   int* base = buf.data();\n   // initial fill rows 0..s-1\n   ...\n   int oldSlot = 0;\n   int newSlot = s % (s + 1); // for i=0, entering row index s; but if s == s? Actually s%(s+1)=s.\n   const int* pold = base + (size_t)oldSlot * n;\n   int* pnew = base + (size_t)newSlot * n;\n\nLoop:\nfor i...\n  ...\n  if next:\n     // read into pnew, update using pold\n     ...\n     // advance slots for next iteration\n     ++oldSlot; if (oldSlot == s+1) oldSlot=0;\n     ++newSlot; if (newSlot == s+1) newSlot=0;\n     pold = base + (size_t)oldSlot * n;\n     pnew = base + (size_t)newSlot * n;\n\nBut careful: after reading row i+s into slotNew, for next iteration i+1 the entering row is i+s+1 and should go to newSlot'=(newSlot+1)%(s+1). The old row for next is i+1 at oldSlot'. So advancing pointers before next loop is correct. However pold currently points to exiting row i; after we finish using it, we can advance. Good.\n\nNeed ensure initial newSlot: For i=0 entering row index s, slot = s%(s+1)=s (since s < s+1). If s could equal? S<N<8000 so s>=1 and s <= n-1; s+1 > s, so fine. But if s == 0 not allowed.\n\nAlso initial oldSlot=0 for row 0.\n\nLet's test with small example mentally:\nn=5,s=3.\nbuf size (4)*5=20 slots rows 0..3.\nRead rows 0,1,2 into slots 0,1,2. col sums.\ncur = sum col[0..2].\ni=0: horizontal scan over ncols=3 windows j=0..2 using cur updates. Then read row 3 into slotNew=s%(s+1)=3%4=3 (empty). pold row0 slot0. Update col to rows1,2,3; deltaFirstS for c<3; cur += delta.\nAdvance oldSlot=1, newSlot=(3+1)%4=0? Wait after reading row 3 into slot 3, next entering row index i+s+1 = 0+3+1=4 should go to slot 4%(4)=0. So newSlot becomes 0. pnew now points to slot0 (which currently holds old row0 but will be overwritten when reading row4). Good.\ni=1: horizontal scan with col rows1..3 and cur for window at column0 of those rows. Then read row4 into slot0, pold should point to exiting row i=1 at slot1. We advanced oldSlot to 1, so pold points row1. Correct.\n\nWorks.\n\nPotential issue: when nrows maybe less than? Always positive.\n\nNow performance considerations:\n- Reading input with getchar_unlocked per int may be bottleneck. Could implement faster fread-based parser. But role A not about I/O; however previous best used \"direct buffer-cursor scanner\" likely custom fast parser. We can include a simple buffered reader to reduce syscalls and improve speed, but that's more of an I/O transformation. The instructions say choose transformations yourself relevant to your role. Memory locality includes reducing memory traffic from input? Maybe not. But we can still use getchar_unlocked; it's already unbuffered stdio with locking removed, decent.\n\n- Loop unrolling: We can unroll the column update/read loop by 4 to improve ILP and reduce branch overhead. Previous best did that. Let's add manual 4x unroll for reading new row and updating col. But careful with n not multiple of 4; handle remainder. This is a loop transformation, allowed under role A? Loop order/unrolling yes.\n\n- Also horizontal scan could be unrolled but current simple loop likely fine.\n\nLet's implement robustly:\n\nfor (int c = 0; c + 3 < s; c += 4) {\n    int v0=readInt(); int v1=readInt(); int v2=readInt(); int v3=readInt();\n    pnew[c]=v0; ... \n    long long d0=(long long)v0-pold[c]; col[c]+=d0; deltaFirstS+=d0;\n...\n}\nfor (int c = (s & ~3); c < s; ++c) ...\nThen for rest from max(s, aligned?) Actually we need first s columns then remaining. We can unroll both segments separately.\n\nBut maybe simpler: read all n values in one loop with 4x unroll and accumulate deltaFirstS only when c<s using conditional? That would add branch per element; better separate loops as current code does (first s, rest). We'll unroll each.\n\nAlso use int for col? Sum can exceed 32-bit? Problem says result fits 32-bit signed but intermediate column sums could be larger? Column sum of S rows each up to maybe 2^31-1 => S*max could overflow 32-bit. Use long long as current.\n\nLet's write final code with:\n- ring buffer size s+1\n- manual slot wrap pointers\n- fused read/update\n- unrolled loops by 4 for reading new row and updating col, both first s and rest.\n- horizontal scan unchanged (maybe add #pragma GCC ivdep? not needed).\n\nNeed include <cstdio>, <vector>. No algorithm.\n\nLet's produce complete file content. Ensure no OpenMP because sequential faster at this scale; previous best likely sequential. We'll remove _OPENMP branches entirely to simplify.\n\nPotential correctness with negative numbers: readInt handles '-'. Good.\n\nOne subtlety: The input rows have whitespace after each integer, including newline? Our parser skips non-digits and handles '-' correctly. It will consume all separators.\n\nLet's write code:\n\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n\nstatic inline int readInt() {\n    int x = 0;\n    char c = getchar_unlocked();\n    bool neg = false;\n    while (c < '0' || c > '9') {\n        if (c == '-') neg = true;\n        c = getchar_unlocked();\n    }\n    while (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {\n        x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n        c = getchar_unlocked();\n    }\n    return neg ? -x : x;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n = readInt();\n    int s = readInt();\n\n    const size_t rowLen = (size_t)n;\n    std::vector<int> buf((size_t)(s + 1) * n);\n    std::vector<long long> col(n, 0);\n\n    // Read first S rows and accumulate column sums.\n    for (int r = 0; r < s; ++r) {\n        int* row = &buf[(size_t)r * n];\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; ++c) {\n            int v = readInt();\n            row[c] = v;\n            col[c] += v;\n        }\n    }\n\n    long long cur = 0;\n    for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += col[j];\n    long long mx = cur;\n\n    const int nrows = n - s + 1;\n    const int ncols = n - s + 1;\n    const int ringSize = s + 1;\n\n    int oldSlot = 0;\n    int newSlot = s % ringSize; // slot for row index s when i=0\n    const int* pold = buf.data() + (size_t)oldSlot * n;\n    int* pnew = buf.data() + (size_t)newSlot * n;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < nrows; ++i) {\n        // Horizontal sliding window over columns.\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n        for (int j = 1; j < ncols; ++j) {\n            cur += col[j + s - 1] - col[j - 1];\n            if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n        }\n\n        if (i + 1 < nrows) {\n            long long deltaFirstS = 0;\n\n            // Read the entering row and update column sums for first S columns.\n            int c = 0;\n            for (; c + 3 < s; c += 4) {\n                int v0 = readInt();\n                int v1 = readInt();\n                int v2 = readInt();\n                int v3 = readInt();\n                pnew[c]     = v0;\n                pnew[c + 1] = v1;\n                pnew[c + 2] = v2;\n                pnew[c + 3] = v3;\n                long long d0 = (long long)v0 - pold[c];\n                long long d1 = (long long)v1 - pold[c + 1];\n                long long d2 = (long long)v2 - pold[c + 2];\n                long long d3 = (long long)v3 - pold[c + 3];\n                col[c]     += d0;\n                col[c + 1] += d1;\n                col[c + 2] += d2;\n                col[c + 3] += d3;\n                deltaFirstS += d0 + d1 + d2 + d3;\n            }\n            for (; c < s; ++c) {\n                int v = readInt();\n                pnew[c] = v;\n                long long d = (long long)v - pold[c];\n                col[c] += d;\n                deltaFirstS += d;\n            }\n\n            // Remaining columns.\n            for (; c + 3 < n; c += 4) {\n                int v0 = readInt();\n                int v1 = readInt();\n                int v2 = readInt();\n                int v3 = readInt();\n                pnew[c]     = v0;\n                pnew[c + 1] = v1;\n                pnew[c + 2] = v2;\n                pnew[c + 3] = v3;\n                col[c]     += (long long)v0 - pold[c];\n                col[c + 1] += (long long)v1 - pold[c + 1];\n                col[c + 2] += (long long)v2 - pold[c + 2];\n                col[c + 3] += (long long)v3 - pold[c + 3];\n            }\n            for (; c < n; ++c) {\n                int v = readInt();\n                pnew[c] = v;\n                col[c] += (long long)v - pold[c];\n            }\n\n            cur += deltaFirstS;\n\n            // Advance ring slots for the next row transition.\n            if (++oldSlot == ringSize) oldSlot = 0;\n            if (++newSlot == ringSize) newSlot = 0;\n            pold = buf.data() + (size_t)oldSlot * n;\n            pnew = buf.data() + (size_t)newSlot * n;\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%lld\\n\", mx);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nCheck unrolled loops: In remaining columns loop, c starts at s after first segment. If s not multiple of 4, remainder handled before entering second loop? Actually we have:\nfor (; c + 3 < s; c += 4) ...\nfor (; c < s; ++c) ... // this handles leftover in first s\nThen for (; c + 3 < n; c += 4) ... starts at c = s after leftovers, good. Then remainder.\n\nPotential issue: if s == n? Problem says S<N so s<n, but could be close. Fine.\n\nMemory allocation size_t(s+1